# the argument of hume-enquiry-4.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Hume, Enquiry XII: the sceptic's first and second objections to the opinion of external existence.
# Main conclusion read as the conditional in S2. S3 is taken as its support.
atom	rest_instinct	the opinion of external existence is rested on natural instinct
atom	contrary_reason	the opinion of external existence is contrary to reason
atom	refer_reason	the opinion of external existence is referred to reason
atom	contrary_instinct	the opinion of external existence is contrary to natural instinct
atom	rational_evidence	the opinion of external existence carries rational evidence with it, to convince an impartial enquirer
atom	qualities_in_mind	it is a principle of reason that all sensible qualities are in the mind, not in the object
atom	bereaved	matter is bereaved of all its intelligible qualities, both primary and secondary
atom	annihilated	matter is in a manner annihilated, leaving only a certain unknown, inexplicable something as the cause of our perceptions
atom	not_worth	the notion is so imperfect that no sceptic will think it worth while to contend against it
P1	S1	rest_instinct → contrary_reason
P2	S1	refer_reason → contrary_instinct
P3	S1	¬rational_evidence
P4	S3	bereaved → annihilated
P5	S3	annihilated → not_worth	kind conceptual
# A1: the step from "all sensible qualities are in the mind" to "matter is bereaved of all its qualities" is left implicit.
A1	-	qualities_in_mind → bereaved
# A2: that an opinion positing such an unknown something is contrary to reason is relied on, not stated.
A2	-	annihilated → contrary_reason
C	S2	qualities_in_mind → contrary_reason	from A1 P4 A2
